1
00:00:00,425 --> 00:00:02,025
This food cost nothing.

2
00:00:02,075 --> 00:00:03,955
And that's why people stopped thinking.

3
00:00:04,333 --> 00:00:05,533
At MIT,

4
00:00:05,583 --> 00:00:07,527
researchers set up a chocolate stall.

5
00:00:07,819 --> 00:00:08,819
A fancy truffle,

6
00:00:08,869 --> 00:00:10,269
for fifteen cents.

7
00:00:10,344 --> 00:00:11,619
A plain little chocolate,

8
00:00:11,669 --> 00:00:12,411
for one cent.

9
00:00:12,780 --> 00:00:14,580
Most buyers picked the truffle.

10
00:00:14,655 --> 00:00:15,855
Seventy-three percent.

11
00:00:15,955 --> 00:00:16,890
Smart choice.

12
00:00:17,349 --> 00:00:17,624
Then,

13
00:00:17,674 --> 00:00:20,016
both prices dropped by just one cent.

14
00:00:20,386 --> 00:00:20,761
Truffle:

15
00:00:20,811 --> 00:00:21,861
fourteen cents.

16
00:00:21,936 --> 00:00:22,826
Plain chocolate:

17
00:00:23,212 --> 00:00:23,717
free!

18
00:00:23,971 --> 00:00:24,445
Free?!

19
00:00:24,781 --> 00:00:25,681
The price gap?

20
00:00:25,756 --> 00:00:26,858
Exactly the same.

21
00:00:27,234 --> 00:00:28,684
But the crowd flipped!

22
00:00:28,784 --> 00:00:30,743
Sixty-nine percent took the free one.

23
00:00:31,212 --> 00:00:33,738
This is called the zero price effect.

24
00:00:33,838 --> 00:00:35,612
Free doesn't just feel cheap.

25
00:00:35,688 --> 00:00:37,355
It feels like you can't lose.

26
00:00:37,820 --> 00:00:39,870
That's why you see free delivery,

27
00:00:39,920 --> 00:00:41,053
if you spend a little more.

28
00:00:41,432 --> 00:00:42,282
Or a free gift,

29
00:00:42,332 --> 00:00:43,226
if you buy two.

30
00:00:43,709 --> 00:00:45,509
So the next time something is free,

31
00:00:45,584 --> 00:00:46,338
ask yourself:

32
00:00:46,768 --> 00:00:48,475
what is it really costing you?

